# Part 2: Core Concepts

This part is in progress. It will explain the main ideas behind the stages that sibl runs. For now, the outline below shows what it will cover.

## Planned topics

- How sibl detects and reads ELF, PE/COFF, Mach-O, and static archives.
- How the global symbol table handles strong, weak, local, hidden, and protected symbols.
- How section grouping and address layout produce output sections and segments.
- How relocations work on x86_64 and AArch64, including range checks.
- How sibl resolves entry points and applies default layout templates.
- How `--place`, `--pin`, `--region`, and `--defsym` change the link plan.

## Until then

Part 1 shows how to build the project, run the inspection pass, and read the report. The [roadmap](/roadmap) shows the remaining phases, including dynamic linking and binary emission.
